  "d2a3abbb-75cd-4fe2-89c6-c25b59f5f1a7": {
   "id": "d2a3abbb-75cd-4fe2-89c6-c25b59f5f1a7",
   "message": {
    "id": "d2a3abbb-75cd-4fe2-89c6-c25b59f5f1a7",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1789877427.716,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Microphones on the headphones pick up incoming ambient sound. A chip generates a sound wave that is the inverse (180° out of phase) of that noise and plays it through the speaker alongside your music. The two waves cancel each other at your ear (destructive interference).\n\nIt works best on steady low-frequency noise (plane engines, AC hum) because those are predictable and the processing has time to keep up. Sudden or high-pitched sounds (voices, clatter) are harder, so the ear cups' passive isolation does more of that work."
     ]
    },
    "status": "finished_successfully",
